The broadcast focuses on the critical humanitarian situation in the temporarily occupied part of the Kherson region. According to Ukrainian Ombudsman Dmytro Lubinets, the Russian side has not yet confirmed its readiness to facilitate the humanitarian evacuation of civilians from Oleshky and the surrounding district.

Ukraine has developed an evacuation procedure and is working with the International Committee of the Red Cross. At the same time, there is currently no safe route for residents to leave. According to the Ombudsman, over 6,000 people — including around 200 children — may remain in Oleshky and the surrounding district. Confirmed data already exists regarding 358 people who urgently need assistance with safe evacuation.

Civilians remain under the constant threat of shelling and drone attacks while facing shortages of food, drinking water, medicine, and other essential supplies. According to Lubinets, out of desperation, some residents are trying to escape on their own, including by crossing mined areas.

Also in this broadcast:
— The situation on key fronts in Donbas;
— Battles for the Sloviansk–Kramatorsk urban agglomeration;
— Latest changes in Ukraine's military command;
— Development of Ukraine's missile program;
— Integration of new anti-missile defense systems.

Watch the main events of Russia's war against Ukraine and the latest crucial news — LIVE.


